      "svg": "<rect x=\"40\" y=\"40\" width=\"720\" height=\"520\" fill=\"#0f172a\" stroke=\"#1e293b\" rx=\"12\"/>\n<g class=\"el\" data-beat=\"2\">\n          <text x=\"400\" y=\"80\" fill=\"#38bdf8\" font-size=\"20\" font-weight=\"700\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Ratios in Maps — The Representative Fraction (RF)</text>\n<text x=\"400\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Look at the lower-right corner of any map — you will see a ratio like 1 : 60,00,000</text>\n<rect x=\"100\" y=\"150\" width=\"600\" height=\"280\" fill=\"rgba(56,189,248,0.05)\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"2\" rx=\"6\"/>\n<text x=\"400\" y=\"180\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\">(map of India)</text>\n<circle cx=\"250\" cy=\"280\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" stroke=\"#fff\"/>\n<text x=\"250\" y=\"265\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"700\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Bengaluru</text>\n<circle cx=\"450\" cy=\"250\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#34d399\" stroke=\"#fff\"/>\n<text x=\"450\" y=\"235\" fill=\"#34d399\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"700\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Chennai</text>\n<line x1=\"250\" y1=\"280\" x2=\"450\" y2=\"250\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\"/>\n<text x=\"350\" y=\"265\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">~3.5 cm on map</text>\n<rect x=\"540\" y=\"380\" width=\"140\" height=\"35\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"rgba(251,191,36,0.15)\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"610\" y=\"400\" fill=\"#fbbf24\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"700\" text-anchor=\"middle\">RF 1 : 60,00,000</text>\n<text x=\"610\" y=\"412\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\">scale</text>\n        </g>\n<g class=\"el\" data-beat=\"3\">\n          <text x=\"400\" y=\"465\" fill=\"#a78bfa\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"700\" text-anchor=\"middle\">What does RF 1 : 60,00,000 mean?</text>\n<text x=\"400\" y=\"490\" fill=\"#cbd5e1\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1 cm on the map = 60,00,000 cm on the ground</text>\n        </g>\n<g class=\"el\" data-beat=\"4\">\n          <rect x=\"100\" y=\"510\" width=\"600\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"rgba(132,204,22,0.10)\" stroke=\"#84cc16\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<text x=\"400\" y=\"535\" fill=\"#84cc16\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" text-anchor=\"middle\">60,00,000 cm ÷ 100,000 = 60 km</text>\n        </g>",
      "beats": [
        "Welcome back, my friend! Today let us look at maps. Have you ever noticed that in many maps there is a ratio given in the lower right corner? It usually looks like 1 : 60,00,000. What does this mean?",
        "This ratio is called the REPRESENTATIVE FRACTION, or RF for short. It shows the ratio between a distance on the map and the corresponding actual distance on the ground. RF 1 : 60,00,000 means that 1 centimetre on the map is equivalent to 60,00,000 centimetres on the ground.",
        "But 60,00,000 centimetres is a huge number. Let us convert it to kilometres. There are 100 centimetres in a metre, and 1000 metres in a kilometre — so 100,000 centimetres in a kilometre. Divide 60,00,000 by 100,000, and we get 60. So 1 centimetre on the map equals 60 kilometres on the ground!",
        "So if Bengaluru and Chennai are 3.5 centimetres apart on this map, the actual geographical distance between them is 3.5 × 60 = 210 kilometres. Remember — this is the GEOGRAPHICAL distance (as the crow flies), not the road distance (which is longer because roads curve around mountains and rivers).",
        "Try this yourself: take any map, find the RF scale in the corner, measure the distance between two cities with a ruler, and multiply by the scale to find the actual geographical distance. Different maps have different scales — but they should all give approximately the same geographical distance. Ready? Let us turn the page..."
      ]
